   121	## 2. A new unconditional lemma: `det L̃∞ ⩽ 0`
   122	
   123	Domrina's Lemma 2.12 (p. 7) is the only root-*location* engine in §2 that
   124	concludes something positive rather than deriving a contradiction, and it is
   125	the engine both repairs below will run.  Its statement:
   126	
   127	> **Lemma 2.12.** Suppose ã ∈ ⟨g̃₁g̃₂⟩.  Put Q₁ := br_ã(g̃₁), Q₂ := br_ã(g̃₂),
   128	> and let Q₃ be the subgraph consisting of all connected components of
   129	> δ(g̃₁g̃₂) incident to ã.  Suppose that `det L̃∞ ⩽ 0`, `det Q₃ > 0`, and
   130	> `det(ãg̃ᵢ) ⩽ (det δ(ãg̃ᵢ))²·det Q₃` for some i = 1, 2.  Then ṽ ∈ Q₁ ∪ Q₂.
   131	
   132	The charged replay types this **REPLAYED-SOUND** (its R-8), and I have
   133	re-verified both displayed applications of [4], (2) line by line; in
   134	particular `det br_{g̃₂}(ã)` inside `br_{g̃₁}(ã)` is `det(L̃ − g̃₁ − g̃₂) =
   135	det L̃∞`, which is where the first hypothesis enters.
   136	
   137	That first hypothesis is *stated, never proved* in the paper: it is discharged
   138	case by case in §§5–7.  Both of my repairs need it at a point where no case
   139	analysis is yet available, so I prove it once and for all.
   140	
   141	> **Lemma DET-LINF.** `det L̃∞ ⩽ 0`, unconditionally.
   142	
   143	*Proof.* Two steps.
   144	
   145	**(i) X̃ − L̃∞ is affine.**  X̃ − L̃∞ = F⁻¹(X − L) = F⁻¹(C²).  F : X̃ → X is a
   146	morphism of projective varieties, hence proper, so F⁻¹(C²) → C² is proper.  Its
   147	fibres are finite: a positive-dimensional fibre would contain a complete curve
   148	C ⊂ F⁻¹(C²); C ⊄ L̃ is impossible because X̃ − L̃ = C̃² is affine and contains no
   149	complete curve, while C ⊆ L̃ with C ⊄ L̃∞ forces C ∈ {g̃₁, g̃₂}, on which F is
   150	non-constant by Proposition 1.2, 1), so C is not contracted.  A proper
   151	quasi-finite morphism is finite, and the total space of a finite morphism to an
   152	affine variety is affine.
   153	
   154	**(ii) Sign.**  Since X̃ − L̃ = C̃² ≅ C², Pic(X̃) is freely generated by the
   155	components of L̃, so the intersection form on the span of the components of L̃∞
   156	is the restriction of the intersection form of X̃, which by the Hodge index
   157	theorem has signature (1, ρ−1).  Hence `A_{L̃∞}` has at most one positive
   158	eigenvalue.  For a nondegenerate symmetric form with p positive eigenvalues,
   159	`det(−A) = (−1)^p ∏|λᵢ|`.  So `det L̃∞ > 0` would force p = 0, i.e. `A_{L̃∞}`
   160	negative definite.
   161	
   162	Suppose it were.  L̃∞ = L̃ − g̃₁ − g̃₂ is connected (L̃ is a tree and the g̃ᵢ are
   163	leaves) and consists of nonsingular rational curves (p. 2).  By Grauert's
   164	criterion L̃∞ contracts analytically to a normal point on a normal compact
   165	complex surface Z, with X̃ − L̃∞ ≅ Z − {finitely many points}.  Any regular
   166	function on X̃ − L̃∞ descends to a holomorphic function on Z minus finitely
   167	many points; by normality and the Riemann extension theorem it extends
   168	holomorphically over those points, and Z compact connected forces it to be
   169	constant.  So `O(X̃ − L̃∞) = C`, contradicting (i), since an affine variety of
   170	dimension 2 has a coordinate ring of transcendence degree 2.  Hence
   171	`det L̃∞ ⩽ 0`. ∎
   172	
   173	Two remarks.  First, this is strictly stronger than anything the paper
   174	establishes; it is reusable in §§5–7, where `det L̃∞ ⩽ 0` is currently
   175	re-derived per case.  Second, it is a *floor*, not an attainment claim: I do
   176	not assert `det L̃∞ < 0`, and Lemma 2.12 does not need strictness.
