   421	## 5. Countermodel analysis: why neither implication is false
   422	
   423	Item (2) of the charge asks, if a repair fails, for a countermodel to the
   424	*implication*.  Both repairs succeeded, but the countermodel space is worth
   425	recording because it explains why the printed one-line proofs were not merely
   426	terse but genuinely under-determined, and because a reader is entitled to know
   427	that "no countermodel" here is a theorem, not a failed search.
   428	
   429	The state space is: a weighted tree L̃ = L̃∞ + g̃₁ + g̃₂ with `det L̃ = −1`, a
   430	distinguished root ṽ ∈ L̃∞, leaf dicriticals g̃₁, g̃₂ with (m,n) = (1,1), (1,2),
   431	a degree function Deg with `Σ_{ã ∈ a⁰} Deg(ã) = 4`, and the target graph L with
   432	Proposition 1.3's determinant pattern.  A countermodel to Corollary 3.8 b)
   433	would be such a configuration with ṽ inside a branch hanging off the interior
   434	of ⟨g̃₁h̃⟩.  Theorem R1 shows the space contains none, and it is instructive
   435	that the obstruction is *not* local: the two branch types available at q̃ by
   436	(F1)/(F2) both have positive determinant, so no local sign argument can kill
   437	the configuration, and indeed the naive route through Lemmas 2.3/2.4 stalls at
   438	exactly that point, because Lemma 2.4's conclusion `det br_q̃(ṽ) < 0` needs an
   439	extra hypothesis over the segment ⟨q̃ṽ⟩ which is unavailable when
   440	`br_q̃(ṽ)` is a lifted U-component (that component may be a nonlinear tree).
   441	What kills the configuration is *global*: the sign of `det L̃∞`, which is a
   442	statement about the affineness of X̃ − L̃∞ and not about the graph at all.
   443	Hence: the implication is true, the printed proof was insufficient, and the
   444	missing ingredient was a global one that no amount of local determinant
   445	bookkeeping would have produced.
   446	
   447	The same remark applies to Lemma 3.15, with the extra wrinkle that its
   448	genuinely awkward configuration (the root inside a lifted U-component at the
   449	fork of Δ) is eliminated not by a determinant inequality at all but by a
   450	counting argument — Riemann–Hurwitz plus (3.1) plus the hypothesis that Δ
   451	misses g̃₁ — which forces h⁰ to be a singleton and then contradicts that same
   452	hypothesis.  A countermodel would have to violate one of (3.1), [4], (4)/(5),
   453	or the non-incidence hypothesis; none is negotiable.
   454	
   455	I record one honest limitation on all of this.  My repairs consume the
   456	structure packages (F1), (F2), (S1)–(S4) as *licensed inputs* — (F1)/(F2) from
   457	Lemma 3.7, typed REPLAYED-SOUND by the charged replay, and (S1)–(S4) from the
   458	undisputed portion of Lemma 3.15's own proof.  I re-derived (S3) but not
   459	(S1), (S2), (S4).  If a later audit overturns any of those, the corresponding
   460	repair must be rerun, not assumed.
