    57	## 1. `DET-LINF-NONPOS`
    58	
    59	Claim reviewed: `det L~_infty <= 0`, unconditionally.
    60	
    61	Verdict: **CONFIRMED**.
    62	
    63	The proof in the repair has three ingredients.
    64	
    65	First, `X~ - L~_infty = F^{-1}(C^2)` is affine.  The equality is supported by
    66	Domrina II, p. 1-2: `X~, X` compactify the two planes, `L~ = X~ \ C~^2`,
    67	`L = X \ C^2`, and `L~_infty = F^{-1}(L)`.  Since `F : X~ -> X` is regular
    68	between compact varieties, the restriction over `C^2` is proper.  Quasi-finiteness
    69	is also checked correctly.  A positive-dimensional fibre over `C^2` would
    70	contain a complete curve.  It cannot lie in `C~^2 = X~ - L~`, since affine
    71	space contains no complete curve.  If it lies in `L~ - L~_infty`, Proposition
    72	1.2 says it lies in one of the two dicritical components `g1~, g2~`, but
    73	Proposition 1.2 also says `F` is non-constant there.  Thus the restriction is
    74	proper and quasi-finite, hence finite; the source over the affine base is
    75	affine.
    76	
    77	Second, the Hodge-index sign step is valid with the determinant convention
    78	used in Domrina II.  The paper defines graph determinants as determinants of
    79	`-A_Q`, where `A_Q` is the intersection matrix.  The whole boundary has
    80	`det L = -1` on p. 3, and the Lemma 2.12 formulas on p. 7 have the same sign
    81	normalisation.  Since `X~ - L~ = C~^2`, the boundary components span the Picard
    82	group.  The intersection form on `Pic(X~)` has at most one positive direction,
    83	so the restriction to the span of `L~_infty` has at most one positive eigenvalue.
    84	If `det L~_infty > 0`, then that restricted form is nondegenerate and has zero
    85	positive eigenvalues, hence is negative definite.
    86	
    87	Third, the Grauert step is legitimate.  If the connected divisor `L~_infty`
    88	were negative definite, Grauert contracts it analytically to a normal compact
    89	surface `Z`.  The complement `X~ - L~_infty` is then `Z` minus finitely many
    90	points.  A regular function on the complement is holomorphic there, extends
    91	across those points by normality, and is constant on compact connected `Z`.
    92	That contradicts the affineness and two-dimensionality of `F^{-1}(C^2)`.
    93	
    94	There is no hidden division by a determinant in this proof.  The argument also
    95	does not assert strict negativity; the conclusion is exactly the floor
    96	`det L~_infty <= 0`, which is all Lemma 2.12 needs.
